WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:08.520
Documentation strings, also known as doctsrings,&nbsp;
are used to document functions, classes, modules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:14.520
and, in some cases, objects. Documentation strings&nbsp;
can also document other important aspects of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.720
within a Python app. In this topic, we want to&nbsp;
see both how to set up documentation strings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.920
and display them when needed, as these strings&nbsp;
can help explain the purpose of an app and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:33.320
items used within the app. For this example, I&nbsp;
have the 414-docstrings file open, and the code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:39.480
is the same as what was generated in the first&nbsp;
review exercise in the course. I want to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.840
a documentation string to the beginning of this&nbsp;
file to explain the purpose of the file. You may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.320
wonder what the difference is between a docstring&nbsp;
and a comment. There are two main differences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:59.400
so watch closely for those differences. Docstrings&nbsp;
are contained within triple quotation marks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:08.360
I will add some blank lines to this code, go&nbsp;
back to line 1, type a triple quotation mark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:20.040
and then add some information about the app.&nbsp;
The first difference between this docstring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:27.280
a comment is that a comment is only good for one&nbsp;
line, while a docstring can take multiple lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:36.400
Docstrings should ideally follow PEP 257, Python&nbsp;
Enhancement Proposal, which provides conventions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.640
for writing docstrings. The second difference is&nbsp;
this: docstrings can be printed out at any time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:53.040
I will add a print statement on line 15 to print&nbsp;
the docstrings for this file. The code is print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:03.320
and then in parentheses, double underscore&nbsp;
doc double underscore. I will run this code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:12.720
and we see the program output plus the docstring&nbsp;
at the end. And, no, one cannot print comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.440
in the same way in which we just printed the&nbsp;
docstring. Docstrings are important because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.360
documentation is important within a program.&nbsp;
In many cases, programmers may need to fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:29.320
code from an app that was developed weeks ago,&nbsp;
months ago, or even longer ago. Documentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.240
helps one understand a program's purpose and its&nbsp;
code, making enhancements and troubleshooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:41.760
easier to manage. Docstrings are particularly&nbsp;
beneficial for auto-generating documentation.

